\section{Restrictions and score bounds}{

Methods 10, 12, 14, 15, 17, 18, and 20 require exactly two options.
Method 21 requires a single true/false statement with two answer options.
Invalid option counts raise errors when an item is constructed or an Rmd
file converted. Method 21 also requires \code{statement_truth} immediately.
\code{points} scales the published coefficients. Thus methods 4, 7, and 8 can
have a maximum different from \code{points}; \code{\link[=getPoints]{getPoints()}} reports the actual
maximum. Negative single-choice credits remain negative, without a zero
floor. Supporting a preset does not recommend it for every assessment.
}

\section{What statement_truth means}{

Only \code{kanzow_21} needs this setting. \code{statement_truth = TRUE} means that the
assertion posed to the student is actually true, for example "2 + 2 = 4".
\code{FALSE} means it is actually false, for example "2 + 2 = 5". It does not
describe the student's response or the position of the correct option.
For a one-point item, an incorrect response scores -62/38 for a true
assertion and -38/62 for a false assertion. Correct responses score 1;
omissions score 0. Other single-choice methods do not need this setting.
The correct option is still identified by \code{singleChoice(solution = ...)}.
rqti does not infer truth from option labels, which may be translated or
contain HTML. The author must keep \code{solution} and \code{statement_truth} consistent.
}

